#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133540 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "75e33c97-0499-5829-9886-be30b00989ae" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Elements of a Multiple Linear Regression"}] :question/id #uuid "18e10da5-f3e1-52d2-b2c6-2a69a1bf1947" :question/math-academy-id "e-5614" :question/problem "The vector of coefficients of the plane $y=\\beta_0 + \\beta_1 x_1 + \\beta_2 x_2$ that best fits the data above can be found using multiple linear regression as\n\n| $x_1$ | **$1$** | **$2$** | **$3$** | **$4$** | **$5$** |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $x_2$ | **$7$** | **$6.5$** | **$6$** | **$5$** | **$3$** |\n| $y$ | $1$ | $2$ | $5$ | $9$ | $15$ |\n\n$\\begin{bmatrix}β_{0} \\\\ β_{1} \\\\ β_{2}\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nwhere $X$ is the corresponding design matrix and $\\mathbf{y}$ is the observation vector. Find $X$ and $\\mathbf{y}$ in this context." :question/worked-solution "| $x_1$ | **$\\color{blue}1$** | **$\\color{blue}2$** | **$\\color{blue}3$** | **$\\color{blue}4$** | **$\\color{blue}5$** |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $x_2$ | **$\\color{purple}7$** | **$\\color{purple}6.5$** | **$\\color{purple}6$** | **$\\color{purple}5$** | **$\\color{purple}3$** |\n| $y$ | $\\color{red}1$ | $\\color{red}2$ | $\\color{red}5$ | $\\color{red}9$ | $\\color{red}15$ |\n\nThe coefficients of the plane\n\n$$\ny=\\beta_0 + \\beta_1 x_1 + \\beta_2 x_2\n$$\n\nthat best fits the data can be found as the least-squares solution ${\\hat{\\boldsymbol{\\beta}}}$ to the system\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & 7 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 6.5 \\\\ 1 & 3 & 6 \\\\ 1 & 4 & 5 \\\\ 1 & 5 & 3\\end{bmatrix}^(⏞)^(X)\\begin{bmatrix}β_{0} \\\\ β_{1} \\\\ β_{2}\\end{bmatrix}^(⏞)^(β) &= \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 9 \\\\ 15\\end{bmatrix}^(⏞)^(y)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $X$ is the design matrix, $\\boldsymbol{\\beta}$ is the parameter vector, and $\\mathbf{y}$ is the observation vector.\n\nThus, we have\n$\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & 7 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 6.5 \\\\ 1 & 3 & 6 \\\\ 1 & 4 & 5 \\\\ 1 & 5 & 3\\end{bmatrix}$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133541 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5eaee4e9-7be5-5c35-bc88-89697a15d75a" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Elements of a Multiple Linear Regression That Passes Through the Origin"}] :question/id #uuid "ab93579a-f7a1-5cfe-85ce-5a4d5722e7b7" :question/math-academy-id "e-5615" :question/problem "The vector of coefficients of the hyperplane $y = \\beta_1 x_1 + \\beta_2 x_2 +\\beta_3 x_3$ that best fits the data above can be found using multiple linear regression as\n\n| $x_1$ | **$1$** | **$2$** | **$3$** | **$4$** |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $x_2$ | **$-8$** | **$-2$** | **$6$** | **$15$** |\n| $x_3$ | **$8$** | **$6$** | **$4$** | **$2$** |\n| $y$ | $10$ | $20$ | $40$ | $60$ |\n\n$\\begin{bmatrix}β_{1} \\\\ β_{2} \\\\ β_{3}\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nwhere $X$ is the corresponding design matrix and $\\mathbf{y}$ is the observation vector. Find $X$ and $\\mathbf{y}$ in this context." :question/worked-solution "| $x_1$ | **$\\color{blue}1$** | **$\\color{blue}2$** | **$\\color{blue}3$** | **$\\color{blue}4$** |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $x_2$ | **$\\color{purple}-8$** | **$\\color{purple}-2$** | **$\\color{purple}6$** | **$\\color{purple}15$** |\n| $x_3$ | **$\\color{green}8$** | **$\\color{green}6$** | **$\\color{green}4$** | **$\\color{green}2$** |\n| $y$ | $\\color{red}10$ | $\\color{red}20$ | $\\color{red}40$ | $\\color{red}60$ |\n\nThe coefficients of the hyperplane\n\n$$\ny= \\beta_1 x_1 + \\beta_2 x_2 +\\beta_3 x_3\n$$\n\nthat best fits the data can be found as the least-squares solution ${\\hat{\\boldsymbol{\\beta}}}$ to the system\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -8 & 8 \\\\ 2 & -2 & 6 \\\\ 3 & 6 & 4 \\\\ 4 & 15 & 2\\end{bmatrix}^(⏞)^(X)\\begin{bmatrix}β_{1} \\\\ β_{2} \\\\ β_{3}\\end{bmatrix}^(⏞)^(β) &= \\begin{bmatrix}10 \\\\ 20 \\\\ 40 \\\\ 60\\end{bmatrix}^(⏞)^(y)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $X$ is the design matrix, $\\boldsymbol{\\beta}$ is the parameter vector, and $\\mathbf{y}$ is the observation vector.\n\nThus, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -8 & 8 \\\\ 2 & -2 & 6 \\\\ 3 & 6 & 4 \\\\ 4 & 15 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$"}]}
